@node Overview
@section Overview
@cindex agent loop

When you run Codebud with a natural-language request, it goes through
four stages:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Plan} --- The LLM reads your request and the list of available
tools.  It produces a JSON plan: an ordered list of steps, each
specifying a tool name, a description, and the arguments to pass to
that tool.

@item
@strong{Validate} --- The safety engine inspects every step in the plan.
Steps that would run commands not listed in the safety policy are
rejected before anything executes.

@item
@strong{Execute} --- The executor runs each step in order, collecting
the output (standard output, standard error, return code, or file
contents).

@item
@strong{Remember} --- The memory store records what happened so that
future requests in the same session can reference it.
@end enumerate

The @command{run} subcommand performs all four stages automatically.
The @command{plan} subcommand stops after stage 2 so you can inspect
the plan before committing to execution.  The @command{chat} subcommand
repeats all four stages across multiple turns using a single persistent
agent instance.

@strong{Timing:} On CPU-only hardware (for example, an Intel
i7-8550U laptop), the planning stage takes approximately 4--6 minutes
for the recommended 3B model.  This is normal.  Codebud shows a live
progress indicator so you can see that it is working.
(@pxref{Progress Output}).


@node Quick Start
@section Quick Start
@cindex quick start

Before running Codebud, make sure the Ollama server is running and the
model is downloaded.  If you are unsure, run:

@example
codebud doctor
@end example

If all checks pass, send your first request:

@example
OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M \
    codebud run "list the Python files in the current directory"
@end example

You will see a spinner while the model thinks, then a numbered plan, then
the results of each step.  That is all there is to a basic run.

To avoid typing the model name every time, export it in your shell:

@example
export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
codebud run "list the Python files in the current directory"
@end example

Add the @code{export} line to @file{~/.zshrc} (or @file{~/.bashrc}) to
make it permanent.


@node Subcommands
@section Subcommands
@cindex subcommands

Codebud organises its functionality into subcommands:

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .75
@headitem Subcommand @tab Purpose
@item @command{run} @tab Plan and auto-execute a request (most common)
@item @command{plan} @tab Plan only --- inspect without executing
@item @command{chat} @tab Interactive multi-turn session
@item @command{doctor} @tab Check the environment
@item @command{history} @tab Show past step executions
@item @command{sessions} @tab List saved chat sessions
@item @command{models} @tab List downloaded Ollama models
@item @command{config} @tab Show resolved configuration
@item @command{version} @tab Show version information
@end multitable

All subcommands accept @option{--help} for a quick reminder:

@example
codebud run --help
@end example


@node The run Subcommand
@section The @command{run} Subcommand
@cindex run subcommand
@cindex executing a plan

@command{run} is the main way to use Codebud.  It sends your request to
the LLM, receives a plan, and executes every step automatically.

@example
codebud run "@var{your request}"
@end example

Example:

@example
codebud run "create a file called hello.py that prints Hello, world"
@end example

Codebud will:

@enumerate
@item
Show a live spinner while the model generates the plan.
@item
Print a numbered plan summary.
@item
Execute each step in sequence, printing its full standard output
(never truncated --- @pxref{Reading Step Results}).
@end enumerate

If any step fails, Codebud stops, prints the error, and suggests a fix.

@table @option
@item --interactive / -i
Prompt for approval before executing each step, instead of running the
whole plan automatically:

@example
codebud run -i "delete all .pyc files"
@end example

At each step you can answer @kbd{y} (execute), @kbd{n} or @key{RET}
(skip), @kbd{a} (execute this step and all remaining steps without
further prompts), or @kbd{q} (abort).

@item --model / -m
Override @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} for this invocation only.  The model must
already be downloaded --- Codebud never runs @command{ollama pull} on
your behalf.  It validates and warms the model (blocking, with the
usual progress spinner) before planning starts, so a bad name fails
fast instead of wasting a planning cycle:

@example
codebud run -m qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M "your request"
@end example

@strong{Note:} @option{--model} must come @emph{after} the subcommand
name, not before (@pxref{Global Options}).
@end table


@node The plan Subcommand
@section The @command{plan} Subcommand
@cindex plan subcommand
@cindex inspecting a plan

@command{plan} generates a plan and prints it without executing anything.
Use it to check what Codebud @emph{intends} to do before letting it act.

@example
codebud plan "@var{your request}"
@end example

Example:

@example
codebud plan "delete all .pyc files"
@end example

To see the raw JSON instead of the formatted display:

@example
codebud plan --json "delete all .pyc files"
@end example

@noindent
The JSON format is exactly what the executor receives.  Each step has
the fields @code{id}, @code{description}, @code{tool}, and @code{args}.

To also execute the plan after showing it --- printing full,
untruncated step output the same way @command{run} does --- pass
@option{--execute} (or @option{-x}):

@example
codebud plan -x "delete all .pyc files"
@end example

@option{--execute} and @option{--json} cannot be combined: executing
steps has side effects, and @option{--json} output is meant to be
piped into scripts, so mixing the two would be ambiguous.  @command{plan}
also accepts @option{--model}/@option{-m}, with the same validate-then-warm
behaviour as @command{run} (@pxref{The run Subcommand}).


@node The chat Subcommand
@section The @command{chat} Subcommand
@cindex chat subcommand
@cindex interactive session
@cindex multi-turn

@command{chat} opens an interactive session.  Each request you type
is planned and executed, and the memory store accumulates the history
across turns so Codebud can refer to what it did earlier in the same
session.

@example
codebud chat
@end example

After each plan is shown, @command{chat} walks you through every step
one at a time.  For each step you can press:

@table @kbd
@item y @key{RET}
Execute this step.
@item @key{RET} (or @kbd{n})
Skip this step without executing.
@item a @key{RET}
Execute this step and all remaining steps automatically.
@item q @key{RET}
Abort the current plan and return to the prompt.
@end table

To end the session, type @samp{quit} or press @kbd{Ctrl-D}.

On a terminal, a dim horizontal rule is drawn across the full width
before each @samp{[@var{n}] You:} prompt, so turns are easy to tell
apart:

@example
────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[1] You: list the python files
@end example

The rule is omitted when standard error is not a terminal (for example
when it is redirected to a file), and drawn with @samp{-} if the
terminal's encoding cannot show @samp{─}.

@strong{Why use chat instead of run?}  When you are working through a
multi-step task interactively --- for example, exploring a codebase,
then editing a file, then running the tests --- @command{chat} lets
each turn build on the previous one.  Memory from earlier turns helps
the planner make better decisions.

@subheading Slash commands

Any input starting with @samp{/} is handled locally by the REPL and
never sent to the model:

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .75
@headitem Command @tab Effect
@item @code{/help} @tab Print the command reference.
@item @code{/history} @tab Show the conversation turns kept in memory.
@item @code{/clear} @tab Wipe conversation history and reset the turn counter.
@item @code{/undo} @tab Remove the last user+assistant turn pair.
@item @code{/save} @tab Save the session now; @code{/save @var{name}} also
gives it a name to resume by (@pxref{Saved Sessions}).
@item @code{/compact} @tab Replace older turns with a short model-written
summary, keeping the last exchange verbatim (@pxref{Compacting a Conversation}).
@item @code{/doctor} @tab Run environment health checks inline.
@item @code{/model} @tab Show the active model.
@item @code{/model @var{name}} @tab Switch models mid-session.
@item @code{/models} @tab List downloaded models, marking the active one.
@item @code{/plan} @tab Re-display the last received plan.
@item @code{/output} @tab Show the full stdout of the last executed step.
@end multitable

@anchor{Saved Sessions}
@subheading Saved sessions

Every chat is saved automatically to @file{data/sessions/} after each turn
and when it ends; the last line printed tells you how to resume it:

@example
[4] You: quit
  Session saved: 20261025-093012-7f3a  (resume with: codebud chat --resume 20261025-093012-7f3a)
@end example

@code{/save @var{name}} gives the session a memorable name (letters,
digits, @samp{.}, @samp{_} and @samp{-}; unique across sessions), which
can be used instead of the ID.  A chat that ends without any turns is not
saved.  Several chats can run at once; each has its own session file.
To continue a saved conversation, use @option{--resume}:

@example
codebud chat --resume                      @r{# the most recent session}
codebud chat --resume refactor-memory      @r{# by name}
codebud chat --resume 20261025-093012-7f3a @r{# by ID}
@end example

The history is restored, the last exchange is shown so you can see where
you left off, and the conversation keeps saving to the same session.
The session's model is restored too, if it is still downloaded;
otherwise a warning is shown and the current model is used.  An explicit
@option{--model} takes precedence.  Without an argument, @option{--resume}
picks the most recently updated session that is not open elsewhere.

A session can only be open in one chat at a time; resuming one that
another chat has open fails with @samp{Cannot resume: Session
'@dots{}' is open in another codebud chat.}  @command{codebud sessions}
lists what is available (@pxref{The sessions Subcommand}).

@xref{Sessions}, for the file format and how concurrent chats are kept
apart.

@anchor{Compacting a Conversation}
@subheading Compacting a conversation

@code{/compact} asks the model for a short summary of everything except the
last exchange (your latest request and Codebud's answer), then replaces those
older turns with it.  @code{/history} shows the result as a @samp{SUMMARY}
entry:

@example
[3] You: /compact
  Compacted 2 entries (72 chars) into a 80-char summary; kept the last 2.
[3] You: /history
  [1] SUMMARY: The user asked the coding agent to list all Python files ...
  [2] USER: show the last 3 git commits
  [3] ASSISTANT: planned: command(list last 3 git commits)
@end example

Use it when a long session has made the prompt large: a smaller prompt is
faster to process and leaves more room in the context window
(@env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX}).  Compaction cannot be undone; if the model returns
no summary, history is left unchanged.  Codebud also drops the oldest turns
automatically when a prompt would not fit, so @code{/compact} is about keeping
the gist of older turns rather than preventing errors.

@cindex context warning
After each turn, @command{chat} estimates the size of the next planning
prompt.  Once it reaches 70% of the context window, a warning is shown (once
per turn, not after slash commands):

@example
  Context 72% full (~5,900 of 8,192 tokens). Use /compact to summarise older turns.
@end example

With the default @env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX} of 8,192 tokens and a first-turn
prompt of about 1,050 tokens, this only appears after a substantial
conversation.

@code{/model @var{name}} validates the model is downloaded and blocks
on a live warmup (with the usual spinner) before switching --- if
either check fails, the active model is left unchanged and the reason
is printed:

@example
[3] You: /model qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M
| Thinking... 4s (prefill)
  Switched to 'qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M'.
@end example

@code{/models} is the @command{chat}-mode equivalent of the
@command{models} subcommand (@pxref{The models Subcommand}), useful
when you want to see what is downloaded without leaving the session.


@node The doctor Subcommand
@section The @command{doctor} Subcommand
@cindex doctor subcommand
@cindex environment check

@command{doctor} checks your environment and tells you what is wrong
before you try to run an agent call.  Run it any time things are not
working.

@example
codebud doctor
@end example

It checks:

@enumerate
@item Python version (3.10 or later required)
@item @code{agent} package importable (editable install present)
@item @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} environment variable is set
@item Ollama server is reachable on @code{http://localhost:11434}
@item The selected model has been pulled
@item @file{config/harmless_commands.json} and
      @file{config/powerful_commands.json} are valid JSON
@item Enough free disk space near @file{~/.ollama/models/}
@item Enough free RAM for the selected model
@end enumerate

Each failed check prints a one-line fix.  Example output when
@env{OLLAMA_MODEL} is not set:

@example
  [FAIL] OLLAMA_MODEL is not set
         -> Run: export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
@end example

@strong{Run @command{doctor} first} whenever you see a
@code{plan_error} or a connection failure.  It saves time.


@node The history Subcommand
@section The @command{history} Subcommand
@cindex history subcommand
@cindex execution history

@command{history} lists steps that Codebud has executed, across all
sessions, newest last.  Every completed step of @command{run},
@command{plan --execute} and @command{chat} is recorded.

@example
codebud history             @r{# the 20 most recent entries}
codebud history -n 5        @r{# the 5 most recent}
codebud history -n 0        @r{# everything}
codebud history --json      @r{# raw entries, for scripts}
@end example

Example output:

@example
[1] 2026-08-12T14:29:54.237038  command          ok       list files
@end example

Each line shows the time (UTC), the tool used, the outcome (@samp{ok} or
@samp{exit @var{n}}) and the step description.

This is not the same as @code{/history} inside @command{chat}, which
shows the conversation turns of the current session only
(@pxref{The chat Subcommand}).

The entries are stored as JSON Lines in
@file{data/memory/entries.jsonl}, which @command{history} reads without
contacting Ollama.  @xref{MemoryStore}, for the file format and how it is
kept safe against crashes and concurrent writers.


@node The sessions Subcommand
@section The @command{sessions} Subcommand
@cindex sessions subcommand

@command{sessions} lists saved @command{chat} sessions, most recently
updated first, with what you need to resume one:

@example
codebud sessions            @r{# the 20 most recent}
codebud sessions -n 0       @r{# all of them}
codebud sessions --json     @r{# raw summaries, for scripts}
@end example

Example output:

@example
20261025-040741-9dcb  live-check  (open)
  updated 2026-10-25T04:07:41  ·  4 entries  ·  qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
  list the python files in this project

Resume with: codebud chat --resume <id or name>
@end example

@samp{(open)} marks a session that another chat currently has open; it
cannot be resumed until that chat ends.  @xref{Saved Sessions}.


@node The models Subcommand
@section The @command{models} Subcommand
@cindex models subcommand

@command{models} lists all Ollama models that are currently downloaded
and marks which one is selected by @env{OLLAMA_MODEL}.

@example
codebud models
@end example

Example output:

@example
NAME                                       SIZE  SELECTED
  qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M        1.8G  <-- active
  qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M        4.7G
@end example

To switch models, pass @option{--model}/@option{-m} on @command{run},
@command{plan}, or @command{chat}, or type @code{/model @var{name}}
inside an active @command{chat} session (@pxref{The chat Subcommand}).
Both paths validate the model is downloaded and warm it before
switching --- Codebud never runs @command{ollama pull} on your behalf,
and never touches @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} directly on a failed switch.


@node The config Subcommand
@section The @command{config} Subcommand
@cindex config subcommand
@cindex resolved configuration

@command{config} prints the resolved value of every environment variable
Codebud reads, including the defaults it falls back to when a variable is
not set.

@example
codebud config
@end example

Example output:

@example
Codebud resolved configuration:
  OLLAMA_MODEL       = qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
  OLLAMA_BASE_URL    = http://localhost:11434
  EMBED_MODEL        = qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
  CODEBUD_NUM_CTX    = 8192
  OPENAI_API_KEY     = (not set)
  WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED = 0
@end example

Use this to confirm that your exported variables are actually being
picked up by the shell.


@node The version Subcommand
@section The @command{version} Subcommand
@cindex version subcommand

@command{version} shows the Codebud version, the running Ollama server
version, the active model, and the Python version.

@example
codebud version
@end example

Example output:

@example
codebud  0.1.0
ollama   0.17.0
model    qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
python   3.14.4
@end example

Include this output when reporting bugs.


@node Progress Output
@section Progress Output
@cindex progress indicator
@cindex spinner
@cindex prefill phase
@cindex token rate

When Codebud sends a request to the model, it displays a live progress
indicator in the terminal.  There are two phases:

@table @strong
@item Prefill phase (the slow part)
The model is processing your input prompt.  No output has arrived yet.
The display shows a spinning character and an elapsed-time counter:

@example
| Thinking... 47s (prefill)
@end example

On a CPU-only laptop, prefill for the 3B model with a typical planning
prompt takes 200--240 seconds.  @emph{This is normal.}  The model is not
frozen.

@item Generation phase
The model starts producing tokens.  The display switches to a token
counter and speed estimate:

@example
/ Generating... tokens: 42 | 1.1 t/s | 289s elapsed
@end example
@end table

When the model finishes, a summary line replaces the spinner:

@example
prefill: 231s | gen: 58s | total: 289s | tokens: 67 | 1.1 t/s
@end example

@noindent
The progress indicator is written to standard error, so it does not
interfere with piped output.  To suppress it entirely, pass
@option{--no-progress} or pipe stdout to another program (the indicator
is automatically disabled when stdout is not a terminal).


@node Reading a Plan
@section Reading a Plan
@cindex plan display
@cindex reading a plan

After the model finishes, Codebud prints a numbered plan summary.
Example:

@example
Plan (3 steps):
  [1] file_read          read README.md
  [2] command            run pytest -q
  [3] file_write         write test-summary.md
@end example

Each line shows the step number, the tool that will be used, and a
human-readable description.  The tool names correspond to the built-in
tools described in @ref{Tools Reference}.

If the model could not produce a valid plan, you will see:

@example
Plan error: LLM returned invalid JSON
  What happened: The model's output could not be parsed as JSON.
  How to fix:    Switch to a larger model for more reliable JSON.

Tip: run `codebud doctor` to check your environment.
@end example

The inline hint mirrors the @ref{Troubleshooting} chapter.


@node Reading Step Results
@section Reading Step Results
@cindex step results
@cindex execution output

After each step executes, Codebud prints a one-line outcome, followed
by the step's standard output:

@example
  -> ok, 12 lines stdout
line 1
line 2
@dots{}
@end example

Or, if the step failed:

@example
  -> exit 1, stderr: pytest: no tests ran
@end example

@strong{@command{run} and @command{plan -x}} always print the full,
untruncated stdout of every step --- there is no follow-up prompt in a
one-shot invocation to ask for more, so nothing is ever hidden by
default.

@strong{@command{chat}} shows only the first 15 lines of stdout by
default, with a note when more was withheld:

@example
  -> ok, 42 lines stdout
line 1
@dots{}
line 15
  ... (+27 more lines, see /output or --verbose)
@end example

Type @code{/output} to see the rest of the @emph{last} executed step's
output, or pass @option{--verbose} (or @option{-v}) at chat startup to
always show full output for every step in the session.


@node Verbose and JSON Output
@section Verbose and JSON Output
@cindex verbose output
@cindex JSON output

@table @option
@item --verbose / -v
In @command{chat}, show the full stdout of each step instead of the
15-line default preview.  Has no effect on @command{run} or
@command{plan -x}, which always show full output already
(@pxref{Reading Step Results}).

@item --no-progress
Suppress the spinner entirely.  Useful when running Codebud from a
script or when redirecting output.

@item codebud plan --json "@var{request}"
Print the raw JSON plan instead of the formatted display.  Useful for
piping into other tools or for inspecting the exact @code{args} the
planner chose.  Cannot be combined with @option{--execute}.
@end table


@node Global Options
@section Global Options
@cindex global options

@option{--no-progress} and @option{--verbose} can be placed before the
subcommand name:

@example
codebud [--no-progress] [--verbose] @var{subcommand} ...
@end example

@table @option
@item --no-progress
Disable the live spinner and timing summary.  The agent still runs;
only the progress display is suppressed.

@item --verbose / -v
Print full step output instead of one-line summaries.  In
@command{chat} mode this applies to all steps in the session.

@item --help / -h
Print a usage summary and exit.
@end table

@strong{@option{--model}/@option{-m} is different:} it is defined
@emph{per subcommand}, not globally, and must come @emph{after} the
subcommand name:

@example
codebud run --model qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M "your request"   @r{@i{(correct)}}
codebud --model qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M run "your request"   @r{@i{(error: unrecognized subcommand)}}
@end example

This is deliberate, not an oversight: @command{argparse} lets a
subcommand's own default silently overwrite a same-named option
inherited from before the subcommand, so @option{--model} is only
declared where it is actually read (@command{run}, @command{plan},
@command{chat}) to avoid that trap entirely.


@node Environment Variables
@section Environment Variables
@cindex environment variables

All Codebud behaviour is controlled through environment variables.
There are no configuration files to edit.

@table @env
@item OLLAMA_MODEL
@strong{Required.}  The model name exactly as it appears in
@command{ollama list} (and @command{codebud models}).  Example:
@code{qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M}.  Can be overridden per
invocation with @option{--model}/@option{-m} on @command{run},
@command{plan}, or @command{chat}, or switched mid-session with
@code{/model @var{name}} in @command{chat} --- both validate the model
is downloaded and warm it before changing this variable
(@pxref{The chat Subcommand}).

@item OLLAMA_BASE_URL
The base URL of the Ollama REST API.  Default:
@code{http://localhost:11434}.  Change this only if Ollama is running
on a different machine or port.

@item CODEBUD_NUM_CTX
The context window, in tokens, requested from Ollama on every call.
Default: @code{8192}.  Raise it if you work with long conversations or
large files; see @ref{Environment Variables Overview} for the memory
cost.

@item EMBED_MODEL
The model to use for generating embeddings (used by the memory and
search tools).  Defaults to the value of @env{OLLAMA_MODEL}.

@item OPENAI_API_KEY
When set, Codebud falls back to the OpenAI API if Ollama is not
reachable.  The model used is @code{gpt-3.5-turbo} unless overridden
by @env{OPENAI_MODEL}.

@item OPENAI_MODEL
The OpenAI model to use when @env{OPENAI_API_KEY} is set.
Default: @code{gpt-3.5-turbo}.

@item WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED
Set to @code{1} to enable outbound DuckDuckGo web searches.
Default: @code{0} (disabled).  When disabled, the @code{web_search}
tool reports itself as unavailable to the planner.
@end table

The @command{config} subcommand prints the resolved values of all
these variables (@pxref{The config Subcommand}).


@node Backwards Compatibility
@section Backwards Compatibility
@cindex backwards compatibility

Before subcommands were introduced, Codebud was invoked as:

@example
python run_agent.py "your request"
@end example

Both the old style and the new subcommand style still work:

@example
codebud "your request"          @r{(maps to @command{run})}
codebud run "your request"      @r{(equivalent)}
python run_agent.py "your request"  @r{(still works)}
@end example

The old interface's step-by-step approval flow is available as
@option{--interactive}/@option{-i} on @command{run}
